Backport: reject invalid characters in xmlattr attribute names
==============================================================

Target:   jinja2 2.11.3 (tag 2.11.3, pallets/jinja)
Fixes:    CVE-2024-22195  (space in attribute name;  upstream fix in 3.1.3)
          CVE-2024-34064  (also / > = characters;    upstream fix in 3.1.4)

Upstream commits this is derived from:
  7dd3680e  xmlattr filter disallows keys with spaces
  d6550307  disallow invalid characters in keys to xmlattr filter

Why a patch file and not a cherry-pick
--------------------------------------
Neither commit cherry-picks cleanly onto 2.11.3. The conflicts are entirely
era-related, not semantic: 3.1.x carries type annotations, f-strings,
pass_eval_context and d.items(), where 2.11.3 has evalcontextfilter and
iteritems(). The security logic is four lines and is identical.

This patch is the cumulative 3.1.4 behaviour expressed in 2.11.3's idiom, so
all four illegal characters are rejected. That matters: tests/test_cve.py
parametrises over all four, and a space-only backport (3.1.3) fails three of
them in the remediated state.

Verified on Python 3.12.14, x86_64 (ubi9/python-312): the repository's full
test suite passes unmodified in both states -- --expect=vulnerable against
upstream 2.11.3, and --expect=remediated against a wheel built from this patch.

Note re.ASCII makes the patched module Python 3 only. 2.11.3 still claims
Python 2.7 support; irrelevant here, since the target is RHEL 10's Python 3.12.

diff --git a/src/jinja2/filters.py b/src/jinja2/filters.py
index 74b108dc..c397b75b 100644
--- a/src/jinja2/filters.py
+++ b/src/jinja2/filters.py
@@ -205,6 +205,11 @@ def do_lower(s):
     return soft_unicode(s).lower()
 
 
+# Check for characters that would move the parser state from key to value.
+# https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#attribute-name-state
+_attr_key_re = re.compile(r"[\s/>=]", flags=re.ASCII)
+
+
 @evalcontextfilter
 def do_xmlattr(_eval_ctx, d, autospace=True):
     """Create an SGML/XML attribute string based on the items in a dict.
@@ -228,12 +233,24 @@ def do_xmlattr(_eval_ctx, d, autospace=True):
 
     As you can see it automatically prepends a space in front of the item
     if the filter returned something unless the second parameter is false.
+
+    User input must not be used as **keys** to this filter. A key containing a
+    space, ``/`` solidus, ``>`` greater-than sign or ``=`` equals sign can
+    terminate the attribute name early and inject further attributes, so such
+    a key fails with a ``ValueError``. **Values** are escaped automatically.
     """
-    rv = u" ".join(
-        u'%s="%s"' % (escape(key), escape(value))
-        for key, value in iteritems(d)
-        if value is not None and not isinstance(value, Undefined)
-    )
+    items = []
+
+    for key, value in iteritems(d):
+        if value is None or isinstance(value, Undefined):
+            continue
+
+        if _attr_key_re.search(key) is not None:
+            raise ValueError("Invalid character in attribute name: %r" % (key,))
+
+        items.append(u'%s="%s"' % (escape(key), escape(value)))
+
+    rv = u" ".join(items)
     if autospace and rv:
         rv = u" " + rv
     if _eval_ctx.autoescape:
